  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Mild daytime temperatures through Thursday.\n\n-  Patchy valley fog around sunrise each morning.\n\n-  Significant weather change coming Thursday night into Friday.\n\nMild day-time temperatures will continue across the Northern\nRockies through Thursday as high pressure remains in control.  \nStable conditions under the ridge are promoting the development \nof daily valley inversions and trapped cold air, with patchy dense\nfog. Afternoon temperatures will run around 10 degrees above \nnormal for this time of year. \n\nA significant change remains on track late Thursday into Friday, as\na low pressure system and cold front tracks across the region.\nColder temperatures and widespread precipitation, focused along\nand south of the Interstate 90 corridor and along the divide in\nnorthwest Montana are the main story. Precipitation amounts have\ntrended higher over the last 48 hours, as model guidance has come\ninto stronger consensus for a mid-level low to track along the\ndivide in southwest Montana by Friday morning. Widespread\nprecipitation will wrap around the west-northwest area of this low\npointing towards the highest precipitation amounts (0.75-1.50\ninches) along the divide across mountain ranges in southwest \nMontana. As discussed previously, most of the precipitation will \nfall as rain ahead of the cold front, limiting the potential for \nwinter weather impacts. Snow levels will fall to around 5,000 feet\nFriday and lower over the weekend as shower activity begins to \ndecrease. Therefore, forecast snow amounts will be generally under\nan inch across the region and above 5,000 feet. The exception is \nacross higher peaks along the divide, where several inches of snow\nmay fall above 7,000 feet. \n\nThere remains considerable uncertainty in the extended forecast \nas the majority of long-range forecast models have different \nopinions on the overall pattern early next week. Twenty percent of\nthe models show another ridge of high pressure returning to the \nregion, with the other models suggesting a southwest flow or a \ncold Canadian trough. Stay tuned!",
